1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,069
You already know who would say no. You have never asked them.

2
00:00:04,149 --> 00:00:06,230
There was something you needed this month.

3
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:10,200
A favor, an introduction, twenty minutes of someone's time.

4
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:13,820
You did not ask. And you have a word for what that was.

5
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:17,380
Shy. Proud. Bad at needing things.

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,050
But that is not what happened. You were not being modest.

7
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:24,480
You were running a prediction, and it was wrong by half.

8
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:27,880
Researchers call it underestimating compliance.

9
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:30,600
By the end of this you will know the size of the error,

10
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:35,540
why the person you are afraid to ask is calculating something completely different from what you

11
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:40,800
think, and why the way you ask changes the answer more than who you ask.

12
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,480
Start with a clipboard and a street full of strangers.

13
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:51,900
In 2008, Frank Flynn and Vanessa Lake, who now publishes under the name Vanessa Bohns,

14
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:54,740
gave a group of participants a simple job.

15
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:57,880
Go out. Stop strangers.

16
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:00,740
Get five of them to fill in a short questionnaire.

17
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:05,480
Nothing sensitive, nothing expensive, two minutes of a stranger's afternoon.

18
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:09,840
Before anyone left the room, each participant wrote down one number.

19
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:14,340
How many strangers do you think you will have to approach before five of them say yes?

20
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:16,440
The average guess was about twenty.

21
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:19,040
The real number was about ten.

22
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:22,000
Sit with that for a second, because it is not a small miss.

23
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:26,120
It is not a rounding error or a nervous person being cautious.

24
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:30,460
Half of the people the participants had already written off in advance said yes.

25
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:34,100
Every one of those was a stranger who would have helped and was never asked,

26
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:38,880
and the participant walked past them holding a number they had invented in a room upstairs.

27
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,260
Flynn and Lake ran it again with a different ask.

28
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:45,540
Borrow a stranger's phone to make a call. The guess came in high again.

29
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:48,240
They ran it with a request that costs real time,

30
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:51,160
asking a stranger to walk them across campus to the gym.

31
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:54,700
High again. The shape of the error did not move.

32
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:58,700
Whatever the favor was, people expected to be turned down roughly twice as often as they

33
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:01,500
actually were. So what is the error made of?

34
00:02:01,580 --> 00:02:04,250
Not shyness. Something more mechanical than that.

35
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:07,380
When you plan an ask, you sit on one side of it,

36
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:10,900
and from that side exactly one thing is visible.

37
00:02:11,109 --> 00:02:14,380
The cost of the yes. How many minutes it takes them.

38
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:18,660
How inconvenient it is. How much of a nuisance you are about to be.

39
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:22,629
You run that number carefully, because it is the only number you can see,

40
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:25,440
and then you decide it is too high.

41
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:29,700
The person you are about to ask is not running that number at all.

42
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:35,520
They are running the cost of the no. To refuse you, they have to be a person who refused you.

43
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:40,640
Out loud, in their own voice, while you stand there having just said what you needed.

44
00:02:41,049 --> 00:02:45,860
That is not a calculation about time. That is a calculation about who they are going to be for

45
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:49,960
the next ten seconds, and it is expensive in a way that two minutes of filling in a

46
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:53,920
questionnaire is not. Two people, two different sums,

47
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,600
and only one of them is being solved by the person who decides whether the ask happens.

48
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:02,820
Bohns spent the following years documenting how far this goes.

49
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:08,500
The same gap shows up when people ask for donations, for signatures, for help at work.

50
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:11,120
It runs the other way too, which is less comfortable:

51
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:15,580
people also underestimate how much pressure they put on someone when they ask for something they

52
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:20,140
should not have asked for. The instrument is not tuned for modesty.

53
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:26,560
It is simply not tuned. Nicholas Epley and Juliana Schroeder found the same misreading a step

54
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:29,240
earlier, before the ask exists at all.

55
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:34,040
They took commuters on trains and buses in Chicago and asked one group to strike up a

56
00:03:34,120 --> 00:03:39,220
conversation with a stranger. Another group they asked to predict how that would go.

57
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:42,620
The predictors said it would be awkward and unpleasant,

58
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:45,290
and that the stranger would not want it.

59
00:03:45,370 --> 00:03:49,090
The people who actually did it reported a more pleasant journey than the ones who sat in

60
00:03:49,170 --> 00:03:51,900
silence, and so did the strangers.

61
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:56,060
The forecast was wrong. The experience was fine.

62
00:03:56,350 --> 00:03:59,700
And the forecast is the only part anyone ever acts on.

63
00:03:59,829 --> 00:04:05,600
So why is the instrument set this way, and why would a social animal be built to underestimate,

64
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:10,460
so consistently and in one direction, how willing other people are to help it?

65
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:12,440
Go back to the group it was built for.

66
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:17,759
Lorna Marshall spent years in the 1950s living with the Ju/'hoansi in Nyae Nyae,

67
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:22,940
in what is now Namibia, and she recorded something about meat that sounds strange until you see

68
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:27,500
what it is doing. When a large animal came into camp, it was not given away.

69
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:32,750
It was distributed. Who received which part was fixed by relationship and obligation,

70
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:35,460
worked out along lines that everybody already knew,

71
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:38,700
and the man who made the kill had remarkably little say in it.

72
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:42,380
Generosity was not a decision. It was a procedure.

73
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:47,260
And the accusation people feared was not that they were poor, or weak, or unlucky.

74
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:51,730
It was that they were stingy. Far-hearted, in the phrase Marshall recorded.

75
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:54,360
In a group where everyone eats out of the same day's luck,

76
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:58,940
the person who holds back is the only real danger anyone faces.

77
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:04,510
The anthropologist Nicolas Peterson put a name to the mechanism in 1993,

78
00:05:04,920 --> 00:05:07,280
working across forager societies.

79
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:13,200
He called it demand sharing. In a lot of these groups, goods do not move

80
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:19,380
because the owner offers. They move because the person who needs them asks, directly,

81
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:24,140
out loud, often insistently, and usually in front of other people.

82
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:27,660
Asking is not a last resort or a loss of face.

83
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:31,360
Asking is the channel. It is how the economy runs.

84
00:05:31,610 --> 00:05:33,900
And look at what that does to the arithmetic.

85
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:36,140
In a band of a hundred and fifty people,

86
00:05:36,220 --> 00:05:40,480
the size Robin Dunbar identified as the ceiling for a group where everyone can hold a

87
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:44,380
relationship with everyone, you almost never had to estimate anything.

88
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:49,040
You were not asking a stranger. You were asking someone whose answer you had watched a thousand

89
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:51,440
times, who would be asking you next week,

90
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:54,340
and who would still be sitting at the same fire in ten years.

91
00:05:54,770 --> 00:05:59,300
The odds of a yes were not in doubt. They were not even a question.

92
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:01,920
So the mind was never issued a compliance estimator.

93
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:06,280
It did not need one. What it needed was the opposite instrument.

94
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:10,810
A brake. Because in a small group where nothing is private and nobody leaves,

95
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:14,780
the thing that could actually ruin you was never being refused.

96
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:17,820
It was becoming known as the one who always needs.

97
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:22,920
Ask too often, ask too large, ask without ever being on the other side of it,

98
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,440
and the group has a word for you, and the word travels, and it travels faster than you do.

99
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:32,180
That brake is the feeling you get in the half second before you decide not to send the message.

100
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:37,060
It is not a forecast about them. It has never once been a forecast about them.

101
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:41,060
It is a very old instrument measuring your own standing, and it was accurate,

102
00:06:41,140 --> 00:06:45,820
and it was necessary, and it kept your ancestors inside the only group that was keeping them

103
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:50,250
alive. Here is what changed, and it is not human generosity.

104
00:06:50,580 --> 00:06:53,840
In Nyae Nyae, the network was on by default.

105
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:58,460
Meat arrived whether or not you asked for it, because the rules moved it toward you.

106
00:06:58,580 --> 00:07:03,320
The brake was safe to leave on, because leaving it on cost you almost nothing.

107
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:07,520
Help was coming anyway. You do not live in that network.

108
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:10,460
Nothing in your life moves toward you automatically.

109
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:14,780
Not the introduction, not the reference, not the twenty minutes,

110
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:18,400
not the hand with the thing you cannot carry alone.

111
00:07:18,620 --> 00:07:22,120
There is only one channel left through which any of it can reach you,

112
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:27,600
and that channel is the explicit request, made by you, to one named person.

113
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:30,460
The network went from on to off.

114
00:07:30,610 --> 00:07:34,900
The brake never moved. And now it runs with no information at all.

115
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:40,040
The person you are afraid to ask is not somebody you have watched refuse a thousand times.

116
00:07:40,190 --> 00:07:45,060
They are a colleague you have known for eight months, or a stranger with a phone in their hand.

117
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:49,920
The brake has no history to read, so it reads the only thing available,

118
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,120
which is how it would feel to be turned down,

119
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:56,220
and it hands you that feeling and lets you file it as their answer.

120
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:59,880
That is the whole error. You are not receiving a no.

121
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,240
You are generating one, and then reading it as though it came from outside.

122
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:06,180
So the shift is not to be braver.

123
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:10,860
Bravery is not the variable, and telling yourself to have more of it has never worked for

124
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:15,970
anybody. The shift is to stop protecting yourself from the awkwardness,

125
00:08:16,260 --> 00:08:18,740
because the awkwardness is the active ingredient.

126
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,820
In 2017, Mahdi Roghanizad and Vanessa Bohns tested the same requests in two channels.

127
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:28,460
Face to face, and by email.

128
00:08:29,060 --> 00:08:35,180
Same words, same favor. The in-person request was roughly thirty four times more effective than

129
00:08:35,260 --> 00:08:39,800
the emailed one, and the people sending the emails had no idea.

130
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:42,320
They expected the two to work about the same.

131
00:08:42,740 --> 00:08:46,500
Read that against the mechanism and it stops being a surprise.

132
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:49,360
The yes was never produced by the size of the favor.

133
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:54,060
It was produced by the cost of the no. And an email costs nothing to refuse.

134
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:58,620
Neither does a message to a group chat. Neither does a hint, or a maybe,

135
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:02,300
or the sentence you have almost certainly used, which is no pressure at all,

136
00:09:02,380 --> 00:09:07,410
only if you have time. Every one of those is a kindness you are doing to yourself.

137
00:09:07,820 --> 00:09:10,160
Each one gives the other person a free exit,

138
00:09:10,280 --> 00:09:14,720
and in doing so removes the exact force that would have produced the yes.

139
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:19,780
So tonight, one thing. Pick the ask you have already decided not to make.

140
00:09:20,080 --> 00:09:22,580
One person, named, not a group.

141
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:25,320
Say it out loud, on a call or in a room,

142
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:29,540
and say the actual thing you need rather than the softened version.

143
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:32,600
Then stop talking and let them answer.

144
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:37,120
Some of them will say no. That is real, and it is not the point.

145
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:40,480
The point is that the no will finally have come from them,

146
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:44,040
and roughly half the time, judging by every study anyone has run on this,

147
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:47,940
it will not come at all. You still know who would say no.

148
00:09:48,020 --> 00:09:52,740
You still have not asked them. But that was never a fact about them.

149
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:58,700
It was a number you produced alone, in your own head, out of the only material you had,

150
00:09:58,780 --> 00:10:03,440
which was the feeling of being turned down. They were never in the room while you decided.

151
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:07,200
Put them in it. And that is the part worth sitting with,

152
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:12,020
because you do this to yourself as well, and the place you do it hardest is the one you look at

153
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:16,960
every single morning. A verdict you wrote in private, in your own handwriting,

154
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:21,280
then spent years reading as though somebody else had handed it to you.

155
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:27,580
Does that sound familiar? There is a video on this channel called The Psychology Of Being Ugly.

156
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:30,800
It is about exactly that. Watch that one next.
